MediatR implements the mediator pattern to decouple request senders from their handlers. 
Controllers send commands or queries to the mediator instead of calling services directly. 
This improves separation of concerns, maintainability, and supports CQRS and pipeline behaviors for cross-cutting concerns 
like logging and validation.

Normally
Controller → Service → Repository
Example
public class ProductController
{
    private readonly ProductService _service;

    public ProductController(ProductService service)
    {
        _service = service;
    }

    public async Task<IActionResult> Create(CreateProductRequest request)
    {
        await _service.CreateProduct(request);
        return Ok();
    }
}

Problems:
	•	Controller tightly coupled to service
	•	Large services over time
	•	Harder to maintain when logic grows


2️⃣ With MediatR

Instead of calling service directly:

Controller → Mediator → Handler
Example
public async Task<IActionResult> Create(CreateProductCommand command)
{
    var id = await _mediator.Send(command);
    return Ok(id);
}

Handler
public class CreateProductCommandHandler 
    : IRequestHandler<CreateProductCommand, Guid>
{
    public async Task<Guid> Handle(CreateProductCommand cmd, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        var product = new Product(cmd.Name, cmd.SKU);
        await _repo.Add(product);
        return product.Id;
    }
}

Now:
	•	Controller only knows Mediator
	•	Business logic lives in handlers

3️⃣ Key Benefits of MediatR

1️⃣ Decoupling

Controller does not depend on service classes.
Controller → Mediator → Handler
2️⃣ Separation of Concerns

Each command/query has its own handler.

Example:
CreateProductCommandHandler
UpdateProductCommandHandler
DeleteProductCommandHandler

3️⃣ Cleaner CQRS Architecture

MediatR fits naturally with Command Query Responsibility Segregation (CQRS).
Commands - 
CreateProductCommand
UpdateProductCommand
Queries - 
GetProductQuery
GetProductsQuery
